<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Palmyrene Egypt</h1><p class="st-meta">270 CE – 272 CE · capital Alexandria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Egypt under Queen Zenobia of Palmyra, held for two years during her challenge to Roman authority in the East.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 270 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>, ruling <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palmyrene-empire">Palmyra</a> for her young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a>, sent her general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabdas">Zabdas</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, helped by the local supporter Timagenes. After fighting with the Roman prefect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenagino-probus">Tenagino Probus</a>, the Palmyrenes gained control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and the province, making it part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyrene-empire">Palmyrene Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Palmyrene regime kept the Roman administration and at first claimed to act within the Empire: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> mint issued coins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaballathus">Vaballathus</a> beside the emperor Aurelian. Control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt's</a> grain gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> leverage over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. By 272 she declared herself and her son emperors.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Aurelian responded in 272, defeating the Palmyrene armies in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> while Roman forces, led according to later sources by the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/probus-emperor">Probus</a>, recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> was captured and Egypt returned to direct Roman rule as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 270 CE – 272 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:65.07%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>270 CE – 272 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Alexandria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zenobia, Vaballathus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman and Egyptian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek, Palmyrene Aramaic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Egypt under Zenobia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=271&amp;focus=palmyrene-egypt" aria-label="Palmyrene Egypt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5452 1041 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Palmyrene Egypt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5772 1297L5972 1183M5772 1297L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5972 1183h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5772 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Alexandria. <a href="/atlas?year=271&amp;focus=palmyrene-egypt">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

